--TEST--
A shift's count is an operand of the operator rather than a bare width, so a mixed-signedness one is refused exactly as `&` refuses its own — `rule:types/arithmetic`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:types/arithmetic`'s `& | ^ ~ << >>` row is "the operand type, preserved", and a
// mixed-signedness pair has no preserved type to be — so `<<` and `>>` judge
// their count by the row their left operand takes, in both directions. That
// is not bookkeeping: `nvs-codegen`'s `emit_shift` reads *one* signedness for
// both of the two things it decides — whether to emit the negative-count
// guard PHP's `Bit shift by negative number` comes from, and whether `>>` is
// arithmetic or logical — and it is this refusal that makes reading one
// sound, a `uint` count having no negative value to guard against.
//
// The count that is not already the operand's signedness says so, with the
// same `as uint`/`as int` every other row of § 4 asks for; a bare digit run
// needs nothing, being placed at the operand's type by the case beside this
// one.
uint $u = 6;
int $i = 2;
echo $u << $i, "\n";
echo $u >> $i, "\n";
echo $i << $u, "\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0407]: `int` and `uint` have no representable common type in arithmetic
%A
error: aborting due to 3 errors
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
